      "question": {
        ":question/id": "b5fbbccb-e1a4-56a2-959c-5c2bb4568e7f",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{6x-5}-3{x}^{3}$ at the point where $x=1.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nLet $f(x)=\\sqrt{6x-5}.$ We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=6x-5.$ Then $f=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (6) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-5}}-9x^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{6}{2\\sqrt{6(1)-5}}-9(1)^{2} \\\\  & =3-9 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
